<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Ms. Barrett Browning called this morning and was seen the same day. For three days she has coughed more, her sputum has gone from about a tablespoon a day to four or five, and it has turned thick and green. She is more breathless on the stairs and feels feverish and tired; her temperature at home last night was 38.0 °C. This morning there were two small streaks of blood in the sputum, no more than that. She has kept up airway clearance twice a day and has started her written exacerbation plan by collecting a sputum sample, which she brought. She has no chest pain or ankle swelling. She takes morphine solution 4 mL (8 mg) twice daily, unchanged since June, and has taken no extra doses and no cough medicine. She is sleeping poorly because of the cough. She finished her sixteen grief therapy sessions last month and feels her mood has held. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Onset</td><td>Three days ago</td></tr><tr><td>Character</td><td>More cough; sputum four to five tablespoons a day, thick and green; two small streaks of blood this morning</td></tr><tr><td>Severity</td><td>Breathless on stairs; highest temperature 38.0 °C</td></tr><tr><td>Context</td><td>Bronchiectasis with Haemophilus influenzae; long-term morphine 16 mg a day during a voluntary taper</td></tr><tr><td>Modifying factors</td><td>Sputum sample collected; airway clearance twice daily; no extra morphine or cough medicine</td></tr><tr><td>Associated signs and symptoms</td><td>Tiredness and poor sleep; no chest pain, wheeze, ankle swelling or confusion</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

