    <code code="11450-4" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Problem List"/>
    <title>Problems</title>
    <text><table><thead><tr><th>Problem</th><th>Clinical status</th><th>Verification</th><th>Code</th><th>Provenance</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  <tr ID="problem-1">
    <td>Chronic cough with daily yellow sputum, occasional streaks of blood, and breathlessness on stairs, worse in winter, for several years after an illness with blood in the sputum about eight years ago; tuberculosis, nontuberculous mycobacteria and bronchiectasis to be distinguished</td><td>resolved</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>R05.3</td><td>S — Synthetic presenting concern at the index visit, superseded by the diagnosis at the consultation; it echoes her documented chest illness (D).</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-2">
    <td>Non-cystic fibrosis bronchiectasis of the left lower lobe and lingula with chronic Haemophilus influenzae infection and mild airflow obstruction (Bronchiectasis Severity Index 3, mild); tuberculosis and nontuberculous mycobacterial disease excluded; synthetic modern diagnosis chosen for a historical chest illness whose cause is disputed</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>J47.9</td><td>S — Synthetic modern diagnosis; the nature of her historical chest illness remains undetermined (R, disputed).</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-3">
    <td>Long-term prescribed opioid therapy (oral morphine 20 mg a day for many years, for cough, sleeplessness and palpitations), with expected physiological dependence; no opioid use disorder on DSM-5-TR assessment</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>Z79.891</td><td>S — Synthetic modern problem anchored to her documented prescribed opium (D); tolerance and withdrawal on prescribed opioids do not count toward opioid use disorder.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-4">
    <td>Prolonged grief disorder after the drowning of her closest brother five years ago, with yearning, self-blame, avoidance of reminders of the sea, and depressive symptoms (PHQ-9 12); no suicidal ideation</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>F43.81</td><td>S — Synthetic modern diagnosis anchored to her documented bereavement (D); not a claim about her real mental health.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-5">
    <td>Vitamin D deficiency (25-hydroxyvitamin D 15 ng/mL) in a woman who rarely goes outdoors</td><td>resolved</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>E55.9</td><td>S — Synthetic finding at intake; repleted by twelve weeks.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-6">
    <td>Underweight (BMI 16.8) with a small appetite</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>R63.6</td><td>S — Synthetic finding at intake.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-7">
    <td>Exacerbation of bronchiectasis (more sputum, purulent sputum, more cough and breathlessness for three days, with low-grade fever), treated with amoxicillin for 14 days and more frequent airway clearance; resolved</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>J47.1</td><td>S — Synthetic intercurrent exacerbation; not a historical claim.</td>
  </tr></tbody></table></text>

