    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Exacerbation of bronchiectasis (J47.1)</content>. Three key symptoms (cough, sputum volume and purulence, breathlessness) worse for more than 48 hours, with fever, a CRP of 38 mg/L and a white count of 11.8: an exacerbation needing antibiotics. She is well enough for treatment at home: oxygen saturation 95%, not confused, eating and drinking, and the blood is only streaking. No signs of pneumonia on examination, so no chest X-ray today. Her last culture grew Haemophilus influenzae sensitive to amoxicillin. [S]<list><item>Sputum culture from today&apos;s sample.</item><item>Amoxicillin 500 mg three times daily for 14 days, adjusted if the culture shows resistance.</item><item>Airway clearance three to four times a day while the sputum is heavy.</item><item>Nurse phone check in two days.</item><item>Call 911 or go to the emergency department for more than a few teaspoons of blood, blood with worsening breathlessness, chest pain, or confusion.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Long-term opioid therapy during a chest infection (Z79.891)</content>. No sedation or slowed breathing; she has taken no extra doses. Morphine also damps the cough she needs to clear sputum, so no extra opioid or codeine cough medicine should be added; the taper step is held where it is until she has recovered. [S]<list><item>Continue morphine solution 4 mL (8 mg) twice daily.</item><item>No cough suppressants.</item><item>Naloxone at home reviewed.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Prolonged grief disorder: safety review (F43.81)</content>. Mood steady; she denies thoughts of death or self-harm; the safety plan and locked box are in place. [S]<list><item>Booster grief session with the psychologist as planned next month.</item><item>988 Suicide &amp; Crisis Lifeline and the clinic number on her safety plan.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: British Thoracic Society Guideline for Bronchiectasis in Adults (2019); CDC Clinical Practice Guideline for Prescribing Opioids for Pain, United States, 2022 (2022); VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for Assessment and Management of Patients at Risk for Suicide (2024)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Nurse phone check in two days; culture result by phone. Seven-month review as planned in October, sooner if not better in a week. Emergency care for more than a few teaspoons of blood, worsening breathlessness or confusion. Next scheduled visit 1845-10-19: seven-month review after an exacerbation: lungs, opioid therapy, mood, safety, weight, and vaccines. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
